.page dmesg 1 print the kernel message log

# SYNOPSIS
*dmesg* [*-c*]

# DESCRIPTION
`dmesg` prints the kernel's log, read from `/dev/kmsg`: the boot messages and whatever the drivers have reported since, each tagged by the subsystem that spoke (Gensokyo, Boundary, Sakuya, Alice, Eiki, Kappa, Satori, Koishi, Keine, Yukari, Yuyuko ...; see gensokyo(7)). The option `-c` is accepted but the log is not cleared.

When the Human Village is mounted read-write from a USB stick (or any disk), the kernel also keeps this log there as `TOUHOU.LOG` in the root of the volume, and the previous boot's as `TOUHOU.PRV`, so a machine without a serial port can be debugged by reading the stick on another computer. A log that does not end with the line about a clean run belongs to a boot that hung. The kernel command line word `bootlog=off` switches this off.

# EXAMPLES
    dmesg | tail -20
    dmesg | grep -i kappa

# SEE ALSO
grep(1), tail(1), gensokyo(7)
